From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jens Willmer <info@jwillmer.de>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:09:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 141/151] Find: ^ and $ per line in every regex search; Replace
 on empty matches; line endings in replacements

- Regex mode always builds with multi_line and crlf, so ^ and $ mean a
  line's start and end in the joined lines too. `\s` no longer makes a
  query search the joined lines (only \n, \r, (?s) or ". matches
  newline"), so `^\s+` and `\s+$` work per line and keep blank lines.
- Replace (also in the joined lines and In selection) inserts at an
  empty match instead of deleting a line break, and skips the empty
  match where the previous Replace ended, as Replace all does.
- A query that names \r keeps the replacement's line breaks as written,
  so \r\n -> \n converts CRLF lines. A match of half a \r\n takes the
  whole ending and keeps the other half (\r -> "" leaves the \n).
  Replace all computes the cursor from the edits, fixing its offset
  per CRLF match.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

